.TH POWERGOV 1 "July 2026" "powergov" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
powergov \- dynamic CPU frequency governor manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powergov
.I command
.RB [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powergov
monitors CPU utilization and switches CPU frequency governors
.RB ( powersave ,
.BR schedutil ,
.BR performance )
to balance power efficiency and responsiveness.
It can run in the foreground, as a background daemon, or as a
.BR systemd (8)
service enabled at boot.
.PP
The daemon applies a three-state machine with implicit hysteresis:
.TP
.B POWERSAVE
CPU load below 25%.
.TP
.B BALANCED
.B schedutil
governor; entered from powersave above 25% load.
.TP
.B PERFORMANCE
.B performance
governor; entered from balanced above 75% load, unless battery-safe mode blocks it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B on
Start the daemon in the foreground (used by
.BR systemd ).
Creates a pidfile and Unix socket for runtime control.
.TP
.B off
Stop a running daemon via SIGTERM using the pidfile, with
.BR pkill (1)
as fallback.
.TP
.B status
Show current governor state, CPU load, battery level, and battery-safe settings.
.TP
.B \-\-battery-safe
.I percent
Enable battery-safe mode: block
.B performance
when battery is at or below
.IR percent
(1\-100).
Use
.I 0
to disable.
Changes apply immediately to a running daemon and are persisted to
.IR /etc/powergov.conf .
.TP
.B \-v
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage summary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/powergov.conf
Persistent configuration.
.TP
.I /run/powergov/powergov.pid
Pidfile for the running daemon.
.TP
.I /run/powergov/powergov.sock
Unix socket for runtime battery-safe updates.
.TP
.I /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_governor
Kernel CPU governor interface (requires root to write).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, missing daemon, or configuration error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start manually:
.RS
.B sudo powergov on
.RE
.TP
Install and enable boot service:
.RS
.B sudo make install-service
.RE
.TP
Set battery-safe threshold to 30%:
.RS
.B sudo powergov \-\-battery-safe 30
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powergov (8),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR cpupower (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Iván Ezequiel Rodriguez.
